---
/**
 * How to report an error.
 *
 * No form, because the site has no backend and no client JavaScript, and a
 * contact form is the one thing that would need both. Reports come by email to
 * an address that exists only for this purpose: it is a Cloudflare Email Routing
 * alias on our own domain, not a personal mailbox, which is the reason it can
 * appear on a public page at all.
 *
 * This page used to route corrections through public GitHub issues. The
 * repository is now private, so that route is gone, and with it the ability for
 * a reader to watch a correction being handled. That is a real loss and the page
 * says so rather than glossing it: the honest version of "public log" is now
 * "published corrections list", below, which is a weaker promise honestly kept
 * rather than a stronger one quietly broken.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const CONTACT = "corrections@aisafetytracker.org";
---

<Base
  title="Corrections"
  description="How to report an error on this site, and what happens to it when you do."
  cites={[]}
>
  <section class="section section--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <h1>Corrections</h1>
        <p class="masthead__meta mono">Reported, fixed, and listed here</p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <p class="lede">
          Something here is wrong. On a site with this many numbers on it, that is a statement
          about statistics rather than about care. The useful question is what happens when
          somebody notices.
        </p>

        <h2>Report it</h2>
        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. That address exists only for this,
          and a real person reads it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Useful things to include: the page, the figure, and what you think it should say. If the
          error is in an upstream dataset rather than in what we did with it, say so, because the
          fix is different and usually involves telling them rather than us. You do not need to be
          sure, and you do not need to be polite about it.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens then</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>An error in our processing</strong> is fixed in the pipeline, and the corrected
            figure appears on the site at the next daily refresh. Where the correction changes what
            a reader would have concluded, it is listed below with the date.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>An error in an upstream source</strong> is not ours to fix. We will note it in
            that source's caveats on the <a href="/sources/">sources page</a>, which is where the
            provenance marker on every affected figure already points, and report it upstream where
            there is somewhere to report it to.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>A judgement you disagree with</strong>, such as how a jurisdiction is coded in
            the Governance Readiness Index, is worth raising even though it is not strictly an
            error. The coding rules and the instruments behind every score are published in the
            <a href="/methodology/governance-readiness/">methodology</a> so that disagreement has
            something to bite on.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Rights holders</h2>
        <p>
          If you hold rights in something published here and want it removed, email the address
          above and we will remove it. This is deliberately easy: every source is declared in one
          registry and every record carries its identifier, so removing a source removes everything
          derived from it in a single change rather than a hunt.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections made</h2>
        <p>
          Nothing yet. Corrections that change what a figure says will be listed here, with the
          date and what changed, once there are any. An empty list on a site this young is more
          honest than a manufactured one.
        </p>

        <h2>What this page can and cannot promise</h2>
        <p>
          It can promise that errors get fixed and that material corrections get listed here. It
          used to promise more: the code and data were in a public repository, so a reader could
          watch a correction land as a dated commit without taking our word for any of it. That
          repository is now private, and the weaker promise is the one this page now makes. Saying
          so is the point. A site whose argument is that you can check where a number came from
          does not get to quietly narrow what it lets you check.
        </p>
        <p>
          What has not changed is the part that does the work: every figure still carries its
          source, its licence and its retrieval date, and still links to the original one click
          away. The check that matters most was always the one that goes to the publisher rather
          than to us.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
